# city_information_modeler layers

    core/      Stateless data and geometry classes. Every public method is parallelizable.
    parallel/  The external runner. Sizes worker pools from config/compute.yaml.
    app/       Orchestration. Imports core classes and composes them in different ways.
    notebooks/ Local testing. Imports core and app, holds no logic.
    config/    compute.yaml and other settings.
    tests/     pytest, offline by default.

`core/` imports nothing from `parallel/`, `app/` or `notebooks/`. Dependencies point inward.

# core/ must be stateless

A core class holds immutable settings and nothing else. Use `@dataclass(frozen=True)` so an
instance is immutable and picklable: a worker process gets a copy and must produce the same
answer as any other worker.

- Never store results, counters, caches or progress on `self`.
- Never mutate an argument. Copy the frame, return a new one.
- No database session, no file read or write, no `print`. Data in, data out.

# core/ methods must be parallelizable

Every public core method declares how an external tool may divide its work, with
`core.contracts.parallelizable`. The decorator only attaches the declaration, so a direct
call still runs the whole job in one process, which is what a notebook wants.

```python
@parallelizable(
    split=SPLIT_SPATIAL_TILES,      # or SPLIT_ROW_BATCHES, SPLIT_SPATIAL_BLOCKS, SPLIT_NONE
    merge=MERGE_CONCAT_DEDUP,       # tiles overlap at their edges, so repeats must go
    workload=WORKLOAD_IO,           # io -> threads; cpu -> processes counted against RAM
    split_arg="boundary_geojson",   # the one argument that carries divisible work
    dedup_on="building_uuid",
)
def fetch_buildings(self, boundary_geojson: Dict[str, Any]) -> gpd.GeoDataFrame:
```

Pick the split by what the work needs, not by what is easiest. `row_batches` is wrong whenever
a row's answer depends on other rows nearby: a method comparing two frames takes them as one
`SPLIT_SPATIAL_BLOCKS` argument, so the runner keeps each row together with the neighbours it
has to see.

To satisfy the declaration a method must take its divisible work in one named argument and
return something the declared merge can recombine. Splitting must not change the answer:
running the pieces and merging has to equal one sequential call, including at the boundary
edge. Check a change with the `sequential` profile against a parallel one.

# Worker sizing belongs to config, never to code

`config/compute.yaml` describes the server: cores, RAM, reserved cores, RAM per worker,
thread count for network work, and the splitting minimums. The runner caps CPU workers by
cores, by the configured ceiling, and by how many the RAM holds, whichever is smallest.

Core code never reads that file, never counts cores, and never starts a worker. Moving to a
bigger server is an edit in `compute.yaml` alone.

# app/ orchestrates, notebooks/ only call

An `app/` module decides which core classes to call and in what order for one use case.
Several orchestrations may compose the same core classes differently; none of them may
reimplement geometry or data logic that belongs in core.

A notebook cell imports from `core` or `app` and calls it. Move anything longer than a call
into core first.
